WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.159 --> 00:00:04.309 align:start position:0%
 
i was 20 years old  when i was invited to join the cleveland  summit  one of the first political events to  feature mostly black athletes  the photos of me sitting with jim brown  bill russell  and the others chose me with a very  serious game phase  oh i was serious all right but inside i  was nervous  and a little scared we were there to  decide whether or not to support the  greatest heavyweight fighter in history  muhammad ali and his refusal to be  drafted based on his moral beliefs  as the youngest and least experienced  member of the summit  who was i to have an opinion on such  life-changing matters  ali himself was only 25 and his decision  to fight the draft would cost him his  career  his reputation millions of dollars  and earned him a prison sentence we  vigorously debated ali's sincerity and  commitment  a few of our group were ex-military and  not sympathetic to draft evaders  but in the end we agreed to support him  to witness ali's  unwavering integrity even as the  government spent years trying to destroy  him  was a turning point for me even though  he had to be scared  he risked everything to do the right  thing  how could i do any less how can any  american do any less  how can any american athlete do any less  after all athletes are trained to face  fear and force themselves to overcome it  that's what i did at the cleveland  summit i remembered my most  fearful moment when i was 17 and had to  run  for my life  it was july 18 1964  a hot muggy saturday during my next to  last summer in new york city  before heading off to a yet to be  determined college i had a summer job  working for a local journal  so i decided to walk over to observe a  harlem protest rally  to see if there was something i could  write about nearly a thousand people  had gathered at the police station to  protest the killing of a  fifteen-year-old unarmed  african-american boy  james powell by a white off-duty police  officer  i never made it to the rally i was still  a couple of blocks away  when i heard the gunshots crack in the  air and saw people screaming in terror  as they ran past me looking for cover  i ran too trying to stay as low as i  could  afraid i was already a target because i  was black  but a more visible target because i was  so tall  as i crouched in fear behind a lamppost  it seemed like i was  never going to make it to that yet to be  determined college  as the crowd became more outraged a  police officer with a megaphone tried to  calm the situation  by commanding go home go home  someone in the crowd shouted back we are  home baby  we are whole hearing that simple phrase  helped me control my fear  and in the years that followed helped me  focus my social activism  i think that's the message we've been  trying to get across all these years in  all these protests  america is our home and we want to feel  valued and safe in our homes  how can we feel valued when we are not  given the same benefits as others  how can we feel safe when 56 years after  the protest of the killing  of that unarmed 15 year old boy we are  still protesting the murders of our  children  does that sound like home to you  we can't go for a walk in the park a jog  in our neighborhood  a drive to the market without fear for  our lives  not just from armed civilians but from  hostile police  does that sound like home our children  can't get the same education health care  or career opportunities as white  children  does that sound like home and some of  our neighbors and leaders  protect monuments that honor and admire  the men who raped  brutalized and tortured our ancestors as  well as rip children for mothers  to sell them on a purely patriotic level  why are we memorializing the traders who  nearly destroyed our country  why aren't these monument huggers asking  for more statues of benedict arnold  everybody's equal among the most  impassioned voices echoing the  we are home sentiment are athletes like  colin kaepernick  lebron james steph curry john carlos and  tommy smith  eric reed megan rapino brandon marshall  and dozens of others they spoke up when  it wasn't popular  now it's more important than ever that  athletes continue to speak up  for everybody whether we want it or not  along with the fame and money  comes the responsibility of being role  models  one study shows that children admire  famous athletes  second only to their parents we need to  earn their admiration by doing more than  winning games  we need to make those kids lives better  by giving them all  a level playing field athletes can't  just  take the money and run hoping others  will take all the risks  that will benefit all of us  [Music]  protest is in the american dna  the united states was born out of  violent public protest  starting with the boston tea party and  every major  social and political advancement in our  history  has been the result of people taking to  the streets to voice their demands  public protests created this country in  1776  and almost destroyed it in 1861.  cities have burned people have been  killed  yet each time civil unrest has forced  the country to take an  uncompromising look at itself to judge  if it's on the right course the course  set by the us constitution  this is what unity looks like it has  been said that the ark of the moral  universe is long  but it bends toward justice what  protesters have discovered  is that the ark of the moral universe  only bends toward justice  when pressure is applied  so stand up kneel down  and speak out so the message rings out  loud and clear across the country  we are home baby we are home  you

